.0.0.0\" in Huawei translates to simply \"0\" in Cisco. The equivalent functionality is achieved by specifying the area with each network statement.', 'trans_template': 'network 172.21.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network 172.22.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]', 'ip ospf [parameter1] area [parameter2]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nrouter ospf 1\\n network 2.2.2.9 0.0.0.0 area 0\\n network 172.21.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n network 172.22.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS, OSPF area configuration is done directly under each `network` statement rather than in a separate `area` subcommand like Huawei. The area ID \"0.0.0.0\" in Huawei translates to simply \"0\" in Cisco. The equivalent functionality is achieved by specifying the area with each network statement.', 'trans_template': 'network 172.22.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0'}]]": 1,
